  "knowledgePattern": "Optimise an absolute-value area over all real parameters",
  "knowledgeTags": [
    "calculus"
  ],
  "solution": "<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the integrand is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. It remains to examine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, together with its endpoints.</p><p>By even symmetry and the crossing at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mrow><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p>Differentiating this expression gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msqrt><mi>t</mi></msqrt></mrow></math>. It changes from negative to positive at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, so that value gives the minimum within the central interval. Substitution yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, below both exterior lower bounds. The global minimum is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>: answer C.</p>",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Find where the absolute-value formula changes",
      "body": "<p>For a fixed t, compare the level t with the range of x squared on the integration interval.</p>",
      "recap": "Split an absolute-value integral where its inner expression changes sign.",
      "pitfall": "The parameter is allowed to be any real number, not only a point in the integration interval.",
      "pause": "Separate the parameter cases and locate the relevant crossing inputs.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l21",
          "reminder": "Split an absolute-value integral where its inner expression changes sign."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evaluate the area as a function of the parameter",
      "body": "<p>Use symmetry where valid and integrate the correct nonnegative height on each subinterval.</p>",
      "recap": "Integrate powers and evaluate the antiderivative at the upper bound minus its value at the lower bound.",
      "pitfall": "A crossing that depends on the parameter also changes the integration bounds.",
      "pause": "Obtain a formula for the area in each parameter case.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "Integrate powers and evaluate the antiderivative at the upper bound minus its value at the lower bound."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Compare interior and boundary minima",
      "body": "<p>Differentiate the area expression in the case that contains a crossing, then compare its possible minimum with the other cases and the boundaries.</p>",
      "recap": "A derivative changing from negative to positive identifies a local minimum; endpoints and other allowed cases still need comparison.",
      "pitfall": "The requested answer is the minimum area, not the parameter attaining it.",
      "pause": "Find the parameter giving the least area, then calculate the area itself.",
      "recall": [
